1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
In 1931, it was the biggest bridge in the Big Apple.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Today, it's grown to become the busiest in the world.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
It's the unfinished masterpiece across the Hudson River

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
and one of the main arteries that keeps the heart of New York City pumping.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, the George Washington Bridge on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:58,506 --> 00:00:59,980
The George Washington Bridge

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,680
was the longest in the world

8
00:01:02,681 --> 00:01:05,260
at thirty five hundred feet in length

9
00:01:05,261 --> 00:01:08,660
the main suspension span was more than double the distance of the previous

10
00:01:08,661 --> 00:01:10,240
record holder

11
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:14,220
the ambassador bridge in detroit michigan

12
00:01:14,221 --> 00:01:18,520
the two-level george washington referred to by locals is the g w

13
00:01:18,521 --> 00:01:22,320
impacts the lives of thousands of motorists and consumers

14
00:01:22,321 --> 00:01:26,240
call new york

15
00:01:26,241 --> 00:01:31,120
We have about 330,000 vehicles a day and over 550,000 people per day.

16
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:34,120
And it really is nothing more than an intersection between two states.

17
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,440
And it's important to the commerce of the two states, not only people getting to work,

18
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:43,800
but about 30,000 trucks a day that have to get the freight and goods from the ports to New England

19
00:01:43,801 --> 00:01:45,460
and to elsewhere in the entire Northeast.

20
00:01:45,620 --> 00:01:50,700
So the George Washington Bridge is an incredibly important link to the economy of the entire New York metropolitan region.

21
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:57,700
To keep up with the tremendous traffic burden, the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey,

22
00:01:57,701 --> 00:02:03,700
charged with running the bridge, has to constantly maintain the 14-lane hanging highway.

23
00:02:03,701 --> 00:02:09,700
In 2002, the Port Authority began one of the largest projects in the bridge's history.

24
00:02:09,701 --> 00:02:15,700
At a cost of nearly $70 million, the bridge is getting a new paint job.

25
00:02:15,701 --> 00:02:27,240
Today, motorists making the world-famous crossing see less of the renowned structural steel and metalwork than scaffolding covered with tarps.

26
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:32,540
Well, here we are at the New Jersey Tower section of the George Washington Bridge.

27
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:38,040
We have been removing all the red lead that was painted on when the bridge was first made.

28
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:43,000
This encompasses erecting this huge, humongous scaffolding and enclosing it.

29
00:02:43,001 --> 00:02:48,200
That's to prevent the red lead from becoming airborne and becoming an environmental problem.

30
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:50,540
This has been going on for a couple of years.

31
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:55,620
Once this is completed, we will move over to the New York side and do the New York Tower as well.

32
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:57,480
It's time-consuming.

33
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,860
There's a lot of steel here, approximately 130 million tons.

34
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:07,900
The Port Authority is going to extremes to keep lead paint from contaminating the river.

35
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:12,900
That separates bustling New York City from the rest of the country.

36
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:22,100
The first attempt to build a bridge across the Hudson was seriously proposed just after the Civil War in 1868.

37
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:29,700
Officials excited by the planned Brooklyn Bridge that would connect the eastern side of Manhattan with Brooklyn

38
00:03:29,701 --> 00:03:33,420
saw the Hudson Bridge as the next logical step.

39
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:40,020
But unlike the Brooklyn Crossing, the Hudson Bridge would be required to carry heavy rail lines

40
00:03:40,021 --> 00:03:42,560
to help bring supplies from other states into the city.

41
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,300
A 3,200-foot span to carry railroad cars, which were very heavy at the time, it hadn't been done before.

42
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:55,080
Even the Brooklyn Bridge, which was being conceived, was really not a major railroad bridge.

43
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:56,652
That wasn't its primary function.

44
00:03:56,653 --> 00:03:59,500
It was going to carry a light trolley and actually pedestrians.

45
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:04,200
But a heavy bridge for railroad cars became much more of an issue, especially the long span.

46
00:04:04,201 --> 00:04:05,580
So it didn't go forward.

47
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:07,700
But the dream wouldn't die.

48
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:11,920
In fact, in typical New York fashion, it only became larger.

49
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,880
The next major step was in 1890 when the North River Bridge Company was formed,

50
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:23,460
and the head of the engineer for that company was a fellow named Gustav Lindenthal,

51
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:28,260
who was a famous engineer at the time and had been building railroad bridges and long-span bridges.

52
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:33,800
Lindenthal was the kind of engineer that we're familiar with from the 19th century.

53
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,860
He thought big, really big.

54
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,660
Even for a New York that likes big, he thought big.

55
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:46,900
Lendenthal envisioned his enormous bridge connecting West 23rd Street and Hoboken, New Jersey, with 12 railroad tracks.

56
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:56,720
The companies that most wanted a bridge across the Hudson were the railroad companies, and they were extremely politically powerful at that time.

57
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:03,500
So part of the reason that Lendenthal thought he could build a monster bridge across such a wide part of the Hudson

58
00:05:03,501 --> 00:05:08,360
was that he had some of the biggest players in American capitalism backing him.

59
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:14,820
But critics pointed out that a massive midtown bridge would eat up too much valuable Manhattan real estate.

60
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:21,100
If you can imagine that you have the lower sides over here on both sides of the river,

61
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:23,800
this being New Jersey, let's say, and this being Manhattan,

62
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:29,260
and then having to build a bridge above the river level some 150 to 200 feet,

63
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:34,880
so boats can pass underneath it, that means that you have to build a rather large approach.

64
00:05:34,881 --> 00:05:41,480
And those approaches to get the bridge traffic up to that level require quite a bit of real estate and taking of land.

65
00:05:41,580 --> 00:05:48,680
And that's always been a major issue in terms of long-span bridges and their approaches in very highly densely populated areas.

66
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:54,860
Even with his powerful connections, Lindenthal couldn't find the financial backing for his vision.

67
00:05:55,560 --> 00:06:00,040
Once again, the bridge became a dream and remained so until 1923,

68
00:06:00,041 --> 00:06:03,060
when the Port of New York Authority was charged

69
00:06:03,061 --> 00:06:06,040
with the responsibility to build a bridge across the Hudson.

70
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:09,640
Again, Gustav Lindenthal returned,

71
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:12,560
but this time his plans were even more grandiose.

72
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:16,220
His new proposed bridge was so large,

73
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:19,560
its two 840-foot towers would be taller

74
00:06:19,561 --> 00:06:21,280
than the tallest building in the city.

75
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:24,680
The 16-lane, 18-track span

76
00:06:24,681 --> 00:06:27,080
would stretch over a half mile

77
00:06:27,081 --> 00:06:30,000
and even have a skyscraper as part of the structure.

78
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:32,580
But times had changed.

79
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:36,060
The once mighty railroads were losing their hold on transportation.

80
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:39,140
And automobile travel was coming of age.

81
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:45,340
With a price tag of $180 million, the bridge was politically impossible to sell.

82
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:51,760
One of Lindenthal's strongest critics turned out to be his own protege,

83
00:06:52,700 --> 00:06:55,260
the Swiss-born engineer, Othmar Amin.

84
00:06:55,261 --> 00:07:00,560
He had worked with Lindenthal a few years before

85
00:07:00,561 --> 00:07:05,060
as his first assistant under the construction of the Hellgate Bridge.

86
00:07:05,061 --> 00:07:10,060
So Lindenthal knew him well, realized him as a reliable person,

87
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:14,300
and for two years they worked on plans for the bridge,

88
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:21,240
and Father slowly realized that Lindenthal's plan was not practical.

89
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:25,940
Both quiet and at 5 foot 4 inches tall unimposing,

90
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:29,760
Amman nevertheless impressed the governor of New Jersey, George Siltzer,

91
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:32,080
with his ability and practicality.

92
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:38,900
Governor Siltzer played an important role in this whole story of the George Washington Bridge

93
00:07:38,901 --> 00:07:41,560
in that he was a good friend of Lindenthal,

94
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:46,400
but behind the scenes was encouraging father to go off on his own

95
00:07:46,401 --> 00:07:51,080
for a bridge that would be more practical.

96
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:55,840
In March of 1923, Amund split from Lindenthal.

97
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:58,940
In a letter to his parents in Switzerland,

98
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:02,380
he complained about the turmoil his former boss had caused

99
00:08:02,381 --> 00:08:04,780
and explained his own plans for the future.

100
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:08,860
In vain, I, as well as others,

101
00:08:08,861 --> 00:08:13,320
have been fighting against the unlimited ambition of a genius

102
00:08:13,321 --> 00:08:16,840
that is obsessed with illusions of grandeur.

103
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:20,200
He has the power in his hands

104
00:08:20,201 --> 00:08:24,600
and refuses to bring moderation into his gigantic plans.

105
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:29,880
Instead, his illusions lead him to enlarge his plans more and more

106
00:08:29,881 --> 00:08:34,940
until he has reached the unheard of sum of half a billion dollars,

107
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:37,880
an impossibility even in America.

108
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:40,920
However, I have gained a rich experience

109
00:08:40,921 --> 00:08:45,720
and have decided to build anew on the ruins with fresh hope and courage.

110
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:52,620
And at that, on my own initiative and with my own plans, on a more moderate scale.

111
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:01,180
Amon had been working on a design for a bridge with six lanes for vehicles

112
00:09:01,181 --> 00:09:05,900
and an additional lower deck for light transit to be added at a later date.

113
00:09:05,901 --> 00:09:11,680
The location for his bridge was further north, around 179th Street,

114
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:14,280
where acquiring land would be less of an issue.

115
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:18,560
Ammon's site offered some other advantages, too.

116
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:23,580
The landmass on both sides of the Hudson River were highly elevated

117
00:09:23,581 --> 00:09:29,200
so that the road deck itself would be many stories above the water level of the Hudson River

118
00:09:29,201 --> 00:09:33,800
and there wouldn't be a navigation problem with tall ships passing under the bridge.

119
00:09:33,801 --> 00:09:38,800
The second factor would be the location that Atmar picked,

120
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:41,980
which was one of the narrowest points at the river,

121
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:45,040
and be able to build a long-span bridge.

122
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:48,540
So it was going to more than double anything that had ever been built.

123
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:52,280
In 1925, with the governor's clout backing him,

124
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:58,020
Amon was hired by the Port Authority as their master bridge designer and chief engineer.

125
00:09:58,021 --> 00:10:07,340
It is hard to describe why Father was able to inspire confidence in people.

126
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:09,760
He had a manner about him.

127
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:16,740
He was a very quiet person, and yet when the time came to say something, it was listened to.

128
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:26,480
The ironic twist in the story of New York's bridges is that the person who wanted to build them didn't basically get to build them,

129
00:10:26,481 --> 00:10:36,267
And the person who was the meek little assistant who came in as an apprentice into the big engineer Lindenthal's office is the one who ended up building most of New

130
00:10:36,268 --> 00:10:37,580
York's famous bridges.

131
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:49,521
The groundbreaking ceremony on September 21st, 1927 was the start of a four-year construction process and the beginning of the career of one of the greatest bridge

132
00:10:49,522 --> 00:10:51,620
builders the world has ever known.

133
00:10:51,621 --> 00:10:53,018
His first challenge?

134
00:10:53,019 --> 00:11:00,640
Finding a rock-solid foundation at the bottom of the Hudson River to support the biggest bridge in the world.

135
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:12,660
According to the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey, 105,942,000 vehicles crossed the George Washington Bridge in 2003.

136
00:11:13,740 --> 00:11:16,900
The George Washington Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

137
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:28,620
Work crews began the epic job of laying the new Hudson River Bridges foundation on October 21st, 1927.

138
00:11:28,621 --> 00:11:40,855
The two most noticeable and structurally significant pieces of the bridge, the towers, needed to be built on firm footings to support the enormous weight of the

139
00:11:40,856 --> 00:11:41,620
structure.

140
00:11:41,621 --> 00:11:46,620
On the New York side, a narrow outcropping of land offered an excellent location.

141
00:11:46,621 --> 00:11:50,660
excellent location.

142
00:11:50,661 --> 00:11:55,940
But on the New Jersey side, the only solid ground was at the bottom of the Hudson River.

143
00:11:55,941 --> 00:12:00,080
They couldn't sit it on land. It would have made the span just beyond what the

144
00:12:00,081 --> 00:12:02,240
Othmar Armand had determined would be

145
00:12:02,241 --> 00:12:05,860
both feasible and physically able to do.

146
00:12:05,861 --> 00:12:09,780
So they had to build the foundation in about sixty to seventy feet of water,

147
00:12:09,781 --> 00:12:12,700
which had not been done before.

148
00:12:12,701 --> 00:12:16,520
In the past, engineers had built smaller underwater foundations with the use of

149
00:12:16,521 --> 00:12:21,712
caissons. Caissons are metal and wood boxes with an open end.

150
00:12:21,713 --> 00:12:23,840
For bridge building, they

151
00:12:23,841 --> 00:12:28,200
are dropped to the bottom of the river like an upside down coffee cup and pressurized

152
00:12:28,201 --> 00:12:33,860
with air to remove the water. Workers then enter into the caisson through an airlock

153
00:12:33,861 --> 00:12:35,719
to excavate or lay cement.

154
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:40,080
But those who spent long hours in the pressurized compartment

155
00:12:40,081 --> 00:12:45,380
were at risk of suffering the bends. Upon leaving the caisson, nitrogen, which over

156
00:12:45,381 --> 00:12:49,280
time dissolved in the workers' bodies due to the pressure, quickly turned back into

157
00:12:49,281 --> 00:12:49,981
gas.

158
00:12:49,982 --> 00:12:55,600
The bubbles forming in the bloodstream were extremely painful and could lead to paralysis

159
00:12:55,601 --> 00:12:56,617
or even death.

160
00:12:56,618 --> 00:13:01,920
To avoid the dangers of caisson construction, Amun and his team of bridge

161
00:13:01,921 --> 00:13:07,420
engineers decided on a different technology to build the New Jersey Tower Foundation.

162
00:13:07,421 --> 00:13:08,431
A cofferdam.

163
00:13:08,432 --> 00:13:15,340
A cofferdam is a watertight shell stretching from the river bottom to the surface.

164
00:13:15,341 --> 00:13:21,640
built, workers pump out the water forming an open shaft to the river bottom.

165
00:13:21,641 --> 00:13:26,920
They were then able to build and construct the foundation for the tower within the dry.

166
00:13:26,921 --> 00:13:30,840
This was a huge economical savings.

167
00:13:30,841 --> 00:13:36,340
Even though bridge engineers deemed the cofferdam to be safer than caissons, building on the

168
00:13:36,341 --> 00:13:40,960
bottom of a river is always a dangerous endeavor.

169
00:13:40,961 --> 00:13:44,920
During the construction of the cofferdam on the Jersey side, three men were working at

170
00:13:44,921 --> 00:13:50,160
the base 70 feet down and unfortunately one of the walls failed and water flooded in.

171
00:13:50,161 --> 00:13:54,800
It flooded the entire cofferdam and these three men unfortunately did drown.

172
00:13:54,801 --> 00:13:59,740
Despite the tragedy, bridge designers still believe the cofferdam was the best and safest

173
00:13:59,741 --> 00:14:02,940
way to build a foundation.

174
00:14:02,941 --> 00:14:06,320
After that tragic accident in the cofferdam, once they repaired that and they were able

175
00:14:06,321 --> 00:14:11,500
to totally unwater the cofferdam again and seal it properly, the seal was so effective

176
00:14:11,501 --> 00:14:14,620
They were able to use a small little pump that you could buy in a hardware store,

177
00:14:14,621 --> 00:14:17,860
and it was enough to keep the water out during the entire time of the construction.

178
00:14:19,380 --> 00:14:23,220
With the water removed, workers resumed their jobs inside the cofferdam,

179
00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:28,500
digging through an additional 20 feet of river silt to reach bedrock.

180
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:31,660
No one had ever gone to that depth before.

181
00:14:32,300 --> 00:14:37,520
And so this was the first time where you saw a cofferdam construction in a major river to that depth.

182
00:14:37,780 --> 00:14:39,760
And after that, it became commonplace.

183
00:14:39,761 --> 00:14:42,620
but it was really a first and it was done at the George Washington Bridge.

184
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:49,000
Finally, workers pumped 37,500 cubic yards of concrete into the dam,

185
00:14:49,700 --> 00:14:56,660
creating an 89 by 98 foot solid foundation for the next phase of building, the tower construction.

186
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:04,580
The design of the two 604 foot towers changed drastically between the planning and construction phases.

187
00:15:04,581 --> 00:15:10,200
Othmar Ammon originally envisioned mammoth masonry and steel structures

188
00:15:10,201 --> 00:15:12,500
similar to those on the Brooklyn Bridge

189
00:15:12,501 --> 00:15:17,960
He as a young man in New York really fell in love with the Brooklyn Bridge

190
00:15:17,961 --> 00:15:21,660
and always held that as a model for the ideal suspension bridge

191
00:15:21,661 --> 00:15:26,160
He felt the stone towers for the George Washington Bridge would be particularly appropriate

192
00:15:26,161 --> 00:15:30,940
because the rock faces of the Palisades on the New Jersey side

193
00:15:30,941 --> 00:15:37,020
and the exposed granite on the Manhattan side would blend with stone towers

194
00:15:37,021 --> 00:15:40,060
as though the towers themselves were part of the natural landscape

195
00:15:40,061 --> 00:15:42,080
that just sort of grew out of the riverbed.

196
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:47,440
But it soon became apparent to Amon that the bridge was being overbuilt,

197
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:50,380
which was a waste of money and materials.

198
00:15:51,380 --> 00:15:55,860
So Amon didn't give up the idea of masonry, but then shifted gears,

199
00:15:55,861 --> 00:15:58,120
hired an architect, Cass Gilbert,

200
00:15:58,121 --> 00:16:05,320
and they began working on schemes to design a stone wall to hang on to the steel framework

201
00:16:05,321 --> 00:16:08,200
that would be there strictly for ornamental purposes.

202
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:16,600
To hold the stone facade, Amann redesigned the interior steel framework into a tight and intricate latticework.

203
00:16:17,720 --> 00:16:23,600
Unknown to Amann at the time, the design would eventually be considered the bridge's crowning architectural achievement.

204
00:16:23,601 --> 00:16:31,580
started in May of 1930 much of the two soaring towers construction was actually done at ground

205
00:16:31,581 --> 00:16:38,660
level the prefabricated steel work was moved into location via barges on the New Jersey side

206
00:16:38,661 --> 00:16:47,760
and trucks on the New York side workers used an erection traveler or movable crane which was set

207
00:16:47,761 --> 00:16:54,200
up between the tower legs and climbed or descended the structure as necessary.

208
00:16:54,201 --> 00:16:59,640
Atop the erection traveler, workers used electric winches to lift the over 20,000 tons of steel

209
00:16:59,641 --> 00:17:05,080
that made up each tower. They then riveted the steel into place.

210
00:17:05,081 --> 00:17:12,580
But in the end, no amount of physical labor or design prowess could finish the towers

211
00:17:12,581 --> 00:17:17,040
As Amon had envisioned them, the bridge that had begun construction in the Roaring Twenties

212
00:17:17,041 --> 00:17:20,080
would be forever undressed by the Great Depression.

213
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:24,220
The circumstances had changed tremendously.

214
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:27,700
We went from building a bridge in the prosperous Twenties

215
00:17:27,701 --> 00:17:33,360
to finishing a public project at a time when the economy had crashed,

216
00:17:33,740 --> 00:17:35,900
people were living in shacks in Central Park,

217
00:17:35,901 --> 00:17:39,900
and people were worried about whether they could put enough food on the table,

218
00:17:40,020 --> 00:17:42,400
let alone have grand public works.

219
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:53,553
As a result, the towers were left bare, and what was originally seen as a cost-cutting measure has become one of the most endearing and celebrated features of the

220
00:17:53,554 --> 00:17:55,720
bridge, its exposed steel frame.

221
00:17:55,721 --> 00:18:02,720
So in the end, by not putting the stone in place, they have revealed a tightly knit,

222
00:18:02,721 --> 00:18:09,720
underlined structure that still conveys the outline of a massive form, but in fact when

223
00:18:09,721 --> 00:18:15,403
you get inside it, it's just three-dimensional lacework. Very pleasing to the eye.

224
00:18:15,404 --> 00:18:16,720
And modern-looking,

225
00:18:16,721 --> 00:18:19,720
the way the Eiffel Tower is modern-looking.

226
00:18:19,721 --> 00:18:25,980
But while one group of engineers was trying to finish its job without stone,

227
00:18:27,180 --> 00:18:31,880
another group was busy making one of the largest concrete blocks the world had ever seen.

228
00:18:32,540 --> 00:18:36,880
On the Manhattan side of the bridge, workers began the epic task

229
00:18:36,881 --> 00:18:41,800
of creating an anchorage worthy of supporting the largest bridge in the world.

230
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:47,040
In 1930, a public contest was held to name the bridge.

231
00:18:47,041 --> 00:18:53,620
Due to an overwhelming response from local school children, George Washington received the largest number of votes.

232
00:18:54,460 --> 00:18:58,020
The George Washington Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

233
00:19:01,860 --> 00:19:08,560
From a distance, the graceful bend of a suspension bridge's cables seem to effortlessly dangle the road above the water.

234
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:16,160
But in reality, the cables are under constant strain to support the bridge's weight and every vehicle that crosses.

235
00:19:17,040 --> 00:19:24,040
To resist the tension, all suspension bridges must have sturdy anchor points.

236
00:19:24,041 --> 00:19:30,040
And as the length and weight of a bridge grows, so must the strength and size of its anchorages.

237
00:19:30,041 --> 00:19:36,040
The design of the anchorages for a suspension bridge are fairly simple.

238
00:19:36,041 --> 00:19:42,040
Essentially, they are designed to resist the pull or the tension in the main cables that comes from the weight of the bridge

239
00:19:42,041 --> 00:19:46,040
and from any live load or wind loads that are applied to the bridge.

240
00:19:46,041 --> 00:19:51,040
Bridge. Traditionally, these anchorages are usually embedded deep into the rock.

241
00:19:51,041 --> 00:19:57,040
In the spring of 1930, builders on the emerging Hudson River Bridge were creating what nature

242
00:19:57,041 --> 00:19:58,973
had not, a good anchor point.

243
00:19:58,974 --> 00:20:03,040
On the New York side of the river, Othmar Ammon, the bridge's

244
00:20:03,041 --> 00:20:08,040
designer, had decided a massive concrete and steel block needed to be constructed to hold

245
00:20:08,041 --> 00:20:11,040
fast the eastern side of the bridge.

246
00:20:11,041 --> 00:20:14,160
There were several sites potentially for the anchorage.

247
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:20,960
One was in Fort Washington Park proper itself, and the other would be up on the Riverside Cliffs.

248
00:20:21,680 --> 00:20:30,080
The Fort Washington Park location would require a very large mass of concrete and masonry structure to be situated within the park.

249
00:20:30,081 --> 00:20:42,003
A lot of the people in the New York area, and specifically up in that area, envisioned this as not being very aesthetic and actually tried to get Amman to move the

250
00:20:42,004 --> 00:20:45,880
anchorage eastward back up onto the riverside cliffs.

251
00:20:46,500 --> 00:20:55,552
This would have resulted in a much longer span to the structure, would have increased the cost of the bridge because the longer span equates to more steel towers, more

252
00:20:55,553 --> 00:20:57,720
foundation costs, more cable costs, etc.

253
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:06,040
Fearing budget overruns, Amon fought hard for the Washington Park location and won.

254
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:10,380
Still, the park was far from perfect for the anchorage construction.

255
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:16,560
Bedrock is significantly below the ground level in that park itself,

256
00:21:16,660 --> 00:21:20,700
so that would have required some extensive work in that park.

257
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:24,180
And what Amon and the designers came up with at the time

258
00:21:24,181 --> 00:21:29,460
was to just basically build a huge weight or block within the anchorage itself

259
00:21:29,461 --> 00:21:32,360
to resist the pull and the forces of the main cables

260
00:21:32,361 --> 00:21:35,540
and therefore reduce some of the excavation costs

261
00:21:35,541 --> 00:21:39,220
that would normally be required for typical suspension anchorages.

262
00:21:40,440 --> 00:21:46,240
With the groundwork laid, bridge workers poured 110,000 cubic yards of concrete

263
00:21:46,241 --> 00:21:53,660
to form a 200-foot wide, 290-foot long, and 130-foot high massive anchorage.

264
00:21:53,661 --> 00:22:03,580
When completed, the mammoth block weighed in at over 260,000 tons, at the time the largest bridge anchorage ever made.

265
00:22:07,740 --> 00:22:10,860
Normally, suspension bridges have two concrete anchorages.

266
00:22:12,120 --> 00:22:16,660
But Othmar Amon recognized a convenient natural substitute on the New Jersey shoreline.

267
00:22:16,661 --> 00:22:23,660
Amann theorized that the New Jersey Palisades into which the bridge was being built were sturdy enough to hold the bridge's cables in place.

268
00:22:23,661 --> 00:22:35,764
When they had to build the anchorage for the New Jersey side, they actually had to dig down into the Jersey Trap Rock, or cliffs, and excavating this was a monumental

269
00:22:35,765 --> 00:22:37,660
task for the time in 1931.

270
00:22:37,661 --> 00:22:38,361
31.

271
00:22:38,362 --> 00:22:45,700
Engineers bored a 150-foot deep tunnel into the cliff face and excavated nearly 200,000 cubic

272
00:22:45,701 --> 00:22:47,173
yards of solid rock.

273
00:22:47,174 --> 00:22:52,920
They dug down and they carried all the excavated rock into these muck cars and

274
00:22:52,921 --> 00:22:58,654
carried them away and then they used the crushed rock in the aggregate for the concrete.

275
00:22:58,655 --> 00:22:59,355
Next,

276
00:22:59,356 --> 00:23:02,764
workers placed steel braces and filled the tunnels with concrete.

277
00:23:02,765 --> 00:23:04,680
The final product was a foundation

278
00:23:04,681 --> 00:23:06,400
as strong as the cliffs themselves,

279
00:23:07,340 --> 00:23:09,480
easily able to hold its share of the weight.

280
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:12,980
With the anchorages in place,

281
00:23:13,560 --> 00:23:15,520
another group of workers began their job

282
00:23:15,521 --> 00:23:17,840
of constructing the four massive main cables.

283
00:23:18,760 --> 00:23:21,500
When strung between the two anchorages and over the towers,

284
00:23:22,240 --> 00:23:25,000
the cables would physically hold the weight of the road below.

285
00:23:29,400 --> 00:23:31,640
Othmar Amon and his design team debated

286
00:23:31,641 --> 00:23:33,980
what type of cable would best suit the GW.

287
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:45,440
Early in the planning stages, steel I-bars, which had successfully been used in the construction of the Queensborough Bridge, seemed a likely choice.

288
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:57,840
At the time, there was a discussion about whether a wire suspension bridge cable was more economical than a chain link or I-bar cable.

289
00:23:58,300 --> 00:24:02,800
I-bars being heavy metal bars that look almost like a chain, a modern-day chain.

290
00:24:02,801 --> 00:24:07,720
and there was some discussion on long-span bridges on which one was more economical to construct.

291
00:24:09,600 --> 00:24:12,700
Since bridge cables don't distribute the weight of the bridge evenly,

292
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:18,200
some sections of the cable experience tremendous loads, while others relatively small loads.

293
00:24:19,220 --> 00:24:24,380
Wire cable is faster to install, but must be uniform in thickness, which wastes material.

294
00:24:25,840 --> 00:24:28,920
I-bars can vary in thickness, according to the loads they will carry.

295
00:24:28,921 --> 00:24:33,640
that means less material is used, but they take longer to forge and install.

296
00:24:34,940 --> 00:24:39,840
Based on the discussions back and forth regarding the type of main cables

297
00:24:39,841 --> 00:24:42,000
to be incorporated for the George Washington Bridge,

298
00:24:43,040 --> 00:24:46,840
Amman and his engineers actually put out alternative designs,

299
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:49,340
and they left it up to the contractors to bid the job

300
00:24:49,341 --> 00:24:51,460
to see which was the most economical solution.

301
00:24:52,420 --> 00:24:53,520
Contractors bid the job.

302
00:24:54,020 --> 00:24:57,080
John A. Roebling and Sons won the job with a low bid,

303
00:24:57,081 --> 00:25:00,940
and their bid was for wire cables, and this is why we have wire cables on the bridge.

304
00:25:02,620 --> 00:25:07,020
The engineers at the John A. Roebling & Sons Company were extremely well-versed

305
00:25:07,021 --> 00:25:09,480
in the techniques of suspension bridge cable construction.

306
00:25:10,340 --> 00:25:13,880
The firm's previous experience included building the Brooklyn Bridge.

307
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:19,480
And they invented aerial spinning and technology

308
00:25:19,481 --> 00:25:22,620
and the methods for aerial spinning as we know it actually today.

309
00:25:23,140 --> 00:25:27,040
For safety and to give bridge builders a place to work high above the water,

310
00:25:27,080 --> 00:25:32,080
The first step in aerial spinning involved building a catwalk.

311
00:25:32,081 --> 00:25:38,080
To begin the catwalk, workers on barges pulled guide wires between the two anchorages.

312
00:25:38,081 --> 00:25:46,080
A team of nine men on a rope gang then used block and tackle to lift the wires to the top of the towers.

313
00:25:46,081 --> 00:25:54,080
Once in place, a trolley was installed on the wires to carry the men hundreds of feet above the water.

314
00:25:54,081 --> 00:26:00,620
Finally, the individual pieces of catwalk were hoisted up the wires or lifted into place from barges far below.

315
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:05,920
With the catwalk finished, the cable spinning could begin.

316
00:26:07,860 --> 00:26:14,160
Electric winches powered two spinning wheels that pulled the pencil-thick individual wires from anchorage to anchorage.

317
00:26:17,340 --> 00:26:20,360
Each main cable contains wires like this.

318
00:26:20,980 --> 00:26:25,300
Each main cable contains 26,474 of these wires.

319
00:26:25,640 --> 00:26:28,540
Each main cable can withstand a pull of 180 million pounds.

320
00:26:30,340 --> 00:26:35,600
Enormous reels, each containing 30 miles of steel wire, were brought in on rail cars.

321
00:26:38,740 --> 00:26:40,360
It was an amazing feat.

322
00:26:40,361 --> 00:26:46,360
The logistics had never been done to that great a height and that great a distance across a river before.

323
00:26:46,361 --> 00:26:57,580
It took 300 workers, 209 days to spin the over 100,000 individual wires of the four main cables.

324
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:04,140
But fully half the work was done, with an eye towards the future.

325
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:11,240
They envisioned an explosion of traffic in the coming years,

326
00:27:11,360 --> 00:27:15,680
and they knew that eventually, in order to meet the demand, that they would need a second deck.

327
00:27:16,360 --> 00:27:19,580
The proposed lower deck had always been part of the plan.

328
00:27:20,340 --> 00:27:23,100
The difficulty for Amann was deciding when to build its cables.

329
00:27:24,900 --> 00:27:31,840
At the time, Amann even considered only designing and constructing one cable to support the one roadway deck.

330
00:27:32,500 --> 00:27:38,860
But based on the geometry of the New Jersey anchorages and the construction methods to build the New Jersey anchorages,

331
00:27:39,300 --> 00:27:43,240
they opted to actually put the second cable up during the original construction.

332
00:27:43,241 --> 00:27:48,480
and that actually proved properly to be a very economical solution.

333
00:27:50,440 --> 00:27:54,240
The final step in cable construction was making four cables

334
00:27:54,241 --> 00:27:57,360
out of the thousands of individual strands of wire.

335
00:27:59,960 --> 00:28:02,860
The main cables are built in a hexagon configuration.

336
00:28:03,420 --> 00:28:05,980
After the hexagon configuration is totally complete,

337
00:28:06,260 --> 00:28:09,260
they are compacted using 12 jacks in a radial configuration

338
00:28:09,261 --> 00:28:11,760
and these wires are then squeezed to a round configuration.

339
00:28:11,761 --> 00:28:16,840
They installed the cable bands, those huge steel casings that you see on the cables.

340
00:28:19,080 --> 00:28:26,620
When finished, the four 36-inch diameter cables weighed in at 28,100 tons

341
00:28:26,621 --> 00:28:30,880
and were ready for the next and final step, receiving the roadway.

342
00:28:30,881 --> 00:28:37,320
The four main cables on the George Washington Bridge

343
00:28:37,321 --> 00:28:42,760
contain 107,000 miles of .196-inch diameter wire.

344
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:46,520
The George Washington Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

345
00:28:50,100 --> 00:28:52,800
From 1927 to 1930,

346
00:28:53,600 --> 00:28:56,440
bridge builders had labored constructing the suspended link

347
00:28:56,441 --> 00:28:59,600
between the west side of Manhattan and the rest of the country.

348
00:28:59,601 --> 00:29:06,600
The final step in completing the George Washington Bridge was hanging the roadway onto the massive cables.

349
00:29:06,601 --> 00:29:09,600
For cost savings and ease of construction,

350
00:29:09,601 --> 00:29:16,600
Othmar Amon, the bridge's designer, took a chance on a design theory that had never been tested on such a large scale.

351
00:29:16,601 --> 00:29:21,600
Just prior to the design of the George Washington Bridge,

352
00:29:21,601 --> 00:29:28,600
engineers had been developing new methods of analysis for long-span bridge structures.

353
00:29:28,601 --> 00:29:30,140
This is the deflection theory.

354
00:29:30,780 --> 00:29:35,020
And the George Washington Bridge was the first very long-span bridge

355
00:29:35,021 --> 00:29:41,200
to really incorporate and take the deflection theory full force on in the design.

356
00:29:42,160 --> 00:29:45,560
The larger a suspension bridge is, the heavier it will be.

357
00:29:46,340 --> 00:29:50,240
According to the deflection theory, once a bridge reaches a certain length,

358
00:29:50,241 --> 00:29:55,000
its own weight will keep it from swaying in the wind or shifting due to traffic loads.

359
00:29:55,001 --> 00:30:01,660
Most bridges utilize a design feature called a stiffening truss to keep the structure rigid.

360
00:30:02,920 --> 00:30:08,400
Steel frames in a box or triangular shape are constructed underneath the roadway to beef up the structure.

361
00:30:10,420 --> 00:30:17,780
Amman theorized that the bridge, in terms of its width of its roadway, which was 106 feet across, which was the widest bridge at the time,

362
00:30:18,040 --> 00:30:23,960
the weight of the cables, you had four large cables weighing some 60 million pounds,

363
00:30:24,680 --> 00:30:27,760
The combination of that and the idea of deflection theory,

364
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:33,320
that it did not require a stiffening truss to stabilize the bridge.

365
00:30:33,321 --> 00:30:35,480
It was rather a bold thought at the time.

366
00:30:36,980 --> 00:30:40,600
The same theory, taken to extremes less than a decade later,

367
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:44,280
would lead to one of the most famous bridge disasters of all time.

368
00:30:45,240 --> 00:30:47,740
With a span of nearly 5,000 feet,

369
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:52,420
the weighty Tacoma Narrows Bridge should have been impervious to wind and traffic loads.

370
00:30:53,280 --> 00:31:00,720
But unforeseen by engineers, the long, extremely thin roadway took on the aerodynamic characteristics of an airplane wing.

371
00:31:01,540 --> 00:31:07,440
As a result, on November 7, 1940, a strong wind caused the bridge to become unstable.

372
00:31:08,320 --> 00:31:13,920
For well over an hour, the center span writhed and contorted before finally crashing to the river below.

373
00:31:15,580 --> 00:31:21,180
The problem is balancing economy and material with stability of structure.

374
00:31:21,181 --> 00:31:32,684
And when engineers, particularly suspension engineers over history, began to push too hard on economy, later down the line, inevitably a bridge would fail, as happened

375
00:31:32,685 --> 00:31:37,300
again and again throughout the history of suspension bridge design.

376
00:31:37,960 --> 00:31:45,600
But for Amon and the George Washington Bridge, the deflection theory gamble and subsequent lack of stiffening trusses paid off.

377
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:50,380
Why did Amon get away with building a bridge that never fell down?

378
00:31:50,381 --> 00:31:56,660
I think part of that is that he had a structural intuition, something that was really visceral.

379
00:31:57,080 --> 00:32:09,660
It's as though his body were the bridge, and he would imagine how the forces of compression and twisting and tension would be experienced by that structure.

380
00:32:10,300 --> 00:32:19,660
And other engineers who followed the same scientific methods that he did and technology designed bridges that failed.

381
00:32:19,661 --> 00:32:27,660
By the spring of 1931, the towers, anchorages and cables of the George Washington Bridge

382
00:32:27,661 --> 00:32:31,660
were ready to accept the crossing's final component, the roadway.

383
00:32:31,661 --> 00:32:36,660
The bridge worker's first step was attaching the suspenders from the main cables.

384
00:32:36,661 --> 00:32:41,660
This is a piece of suspender rope used in the construction of the George Washington Bridge.

385
00:32:41,661 --> 00:32:44,660
This is an actual piece of rope in building the bridge.

386
00:32:44,661 --> 00:32:50,480
This is 2 7 8 inches diameter, contains 271 individual wires.

387
00:32:52,340 --> 00:32:59,380
With the ropes dangling from the main cables, workers used cranes to lift pieces of the roadway's substructure into place.

388
00:33:00,700 --> 00:33:08,700
They actually had to raise up 106 feet long, 56 ton floor beams from the river here and hook it onto each suspender.

389
00:33:08,820 --> 00:33:14,020
After several floor beams were hooked into place, they installed what's called the lower cord.

390
00:33:14,021 --> 00:33:16,260
That connects the ends of the floor beam.

391
00:33:19,580 --> 00:33:25,100
Builders started the road construction at both ends and worked towards the center, eventually meeting in the middle.

392
00:33:29,740 --> 00:33:34,340
The last step involved laying tons of concrete to form the roadway.

393
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:42,400
The original six-lane George Washington Bridge finally opened to traffic on October 25, 1931.

394
00:33:44,020 --> 00:33:50,460
30,000 people witnessed the event, and countless more tuned in on their radios,

395
00:33:50,800 --> 00:33:54,560
as Governor Franklin D. Roosevelt cut the ribbon and opened the bridge.

396
00:33:56,460 --> 00:34:00,660
But the true hero of the day, and the man most responsible for building the bridge,

397
00:34:01,240 --> 00:34:03,180
would remain largely unknown.

398
00:34:04,300 --> 00:34:08,880
Well, I don't think there's any engineer who built more bridges for a single city

399
00:34:08,881 --> 00:34:11,380
than Othmar H. Almond did for New York City,

400
00:34:11,381 --> 00:34:14,000
beginning with the George Washington Bridge.

401
00:34:14,720 --> 00:34:17,560
You know, he went on to design and build the Bayonne Bridge,

402
00:34:17,840 --> 00:34:20,520
the Triborough Bridge, Bronx Whitestone Bridge,

403
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:23,600
Throgs Neck Bridge, and Verrazano Narrows Bridge.

404
00:34:23,940 --> 00:34:25,940
He also designed the Lincoln Tunnels

405
00:34:25,941 --> 00:34:29,160
and oversaw the construction of the Midtown Tunnel.

406
00:34:29,760 --> 00:34:34,240
So even though New Yorkers are very aware of these bridges and tunnels,

407
00:34:34,241 --> 00:34:38,120
very few of them know that one man was the designer for all of them.

408
00:34:38,580 --> 00:34:40,540
Without Amund's bridges and tunnels,

409
00:34:40,541 --> 00:34:43,400
New York City would not be the city it is today.

410
00:34:45,340 --> 00:34:49,020
In the end, the bridge would be seen as a construction anomaly,

411
00:34:49,680 --> 00:34:52,080
coming in both under budget at $59 million,

412
00:34:52,660 --> 00:34:54,660
a full million less than planned,

413
00:34:55,180 --> 00:34:57,440
and ahead of schedule eight months early.

414
00:34:59,360 --> 00:35:01,900
The GW had other impressive qualities.

415
00:35:02,620 --> 00:35:05,220
As traffic demands grew, so had the bridge.

416
00:35:05,900 --> 00:35:08,820
In 1946, it underwent its first expansion.

417
00:35:09,480 --> 00:35:14,020
After World War II, years of stopped automobile production gave way to a car boom,

418
00:35:14,600 --> 00:35:19,180
and the bridge that had given such easy access to the suburbs was now feeling the strain.

419
00:35:21,320 --> 00:35:27,480
The problem is that this commuting culture had the seeds of its own destruction within it,

420
00:35:27,820 --> 00:35:33,700
because the more it became easy to live outside the city and drive in, the more people wanted to do it.

421
00:35:33,820 --> 00:35:37,160
The more people wanted to do it, the less easy it became to drive in.

422
00:35:37,161 --> 00:35:42,460
At completion, the center section of the bridge had remained an open steel grid.

423
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:47,500
The paving of the center two lanes increased the bridge's capacity by a third,

424
00:35:48,120 --> 00:35:51,440
but the bridge wouldn't truly be finished for another 13 years.

425
00:35:52,500 --> 00:35:57,280
In 1959, the George Washington Bridge would begin its final metamorphosis,

426
00:35:57,940 --> 00:36:02,940
as massive increases in traffic warranted the building of another entire level of road surface.

427
00:36:02,941 --> 00:36:09,360
Fortunately, Othmar Amund's foresight would again come into play.

428
00:36:10,360 --> 00:36:15,780
The two additional main cables, spun during the original construction, would hold the new deck.

429
00:36:17,400 --> 00:36:23,000
The original design showed two lanes of light rail on the lower deck.

430
00:36:23,220 --> 00:36:27,680
But as time wore on, the automobile became more and more a prominent means of transportation.

431
00:36:27,960 --> 00:36:31,000
So then they decided that we're not going to use streetcars.

432
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:32,720
We're going to design it for vehicular traffic.

433
00:36:32,940 --> 00:36:35,360
Admar Amman was a visionary in many ways,

434
00:36:35,361 --> 00:36:40,040
and one of the many ways that we later New Yorkers can appreciate his vision

435
00:36:40,041 --> 00:36:46,840
is that even in the 1920s, he realized that there might be a need for more lanes on that bridge,

436
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:51,780
and he built capacity into it so that it would be possible to build another deck in.

437
00:36:53,460 --> 00:36:57,660
Workers hoisted 76 road segments from barges on the river below

438
00:36:57,661 --> 00:37:00,960
and connected them to the suspender cables.

439
00:37:02,940 --> 00:37:07,180
Next, they fastened the two decks together using thousands of bolts.

440
00:37:09,900 --> 00:37:16,700
By phasing construction to be in pace with demand,

441
00:37:17,040 --> 00:37:20,660
they were able to build a less expensive bridge initially.

442
00:37:21,660 --> 00:37:24,600
And then as revenues came in from tolls for the bridge,

443
00:37:24,601 --> 00:37:29,900
they were able to set aside money to do construction at a future date.

444
00:37:29,901 --> 00:37:38,400
Finished in 1963 at a cost of nearly $80 million, the construction marked the end of building on the GW.

445
00:37:39,120 --> 00:37:45,920
With the final renovation and additional traffic capacity, the bridge was poised to take the title as busiest in the world.

446
00:37:46,820 --> 00:37:52,760
Today, with nearly half a million people crossing every 24 hours, the bridge has donned that mantle.

447
00:37:53,680 --> 00:37:58,920
But what most customers don't realize is that the GW is more than just steel and concrete.

448
00:37:58,921 --> 00:38:03,600
It takes some cutting-edge technology to keep all that traffic moving.

449
00:38:06,860 --> 00:38:12,380
In December of 1965, Amund's theory about the bridge's strength was put to the test

450
00:38:12,381 --> 00:38:16,280
when a private plane crashed on the upper deck of the George Washington Bridge.

451
00:38:16,700 --> 00:38:19,320
Both the bridge and pilot were unharmed.

452
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:22,860
The George Washington Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

453
00:38:22,861 --> 00:38:29,400
The George Washington Bridge is still the busiest in the world.

454
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:33,380
With hundreds of thousands of cars and trucks crossing every day,

455
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:40,020
the Port Authority relies on a small army of bridge personnel to keep the traffic moving and the bridge safe.

456
00:38:40,900 --> 00:38:47,560
All told, there's about 350 people that perform various functions at the bridge,

457
00:38:47,620 --> 00:38:51,360
whether it's toll collection, emergency response, maintenance of the facility,

458
00:38:51,361 --> 00:38:55,720
and then the administration of all of those people scheduling timekeeping.

459
00:38:56,060 --> 00:39:00,460
And then on top of that, there's about 50 police officers with their supervisory structure

460
00:39:00,461 --> 00:39:02,620
that keeps the bridge open 24-7.

461
00:39:04,920 --> 00:39:08,480
Ten years ago, if a car broke down on the George Washington Bridge,

462
00:39:08,960 --> 00:39:12,480
the Port Authority would have to wait for the stranded motorist to contact them.

463
00:39:13,140 --> 00:39:14,940
Today, the bridge is wired,

464
00:39:15,460 --> 00:39:20,300
and the new technology allows the Port Authority to keep tabs on all aspects of the operation.

465
00:39:21,360 --> 00:39:27,040
One of the great tools that we have here at the George Washington Bridge is the intelligent

466
00:39:27,041 --> 00:39:28,360
transportation system.

467
00:39:28,361 --> 00:39:31,480
It's a computer operating system.

468
00:39:31,481 --> 00:39:36,120
One of the things that it does for us, it gives us information regarding our roadway

469
00:39:36,121 --> 00:39:37,120
conditions.

470
00:39:37,121 --> 00:39:42,000
We have roadway sensors strategically located throughout our roadway.

471
00:39:42,001 --> 00:39:44,480
They're embedded underneath the asphalt for protection.

472
00:39:44,481 --> 00:39:46,880
They give us two things.

473
00:39:46,881 --> 00:39:52,220
One is basically weather, especially in wintertime, when it comes to freezing roadways.

474
00:39:52,580 --> 00:39:56,100
The other thing it does for us is it gives us an indication of traffic flow

475
00:39:56,101 --> 00:40:00,900
and if there's any kind of stoppage to that flow, for instance, an accident or a disabled vehicle.

476
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:08,320
Once a problem has been detected, bridge personnel can immediately get a good look at the situation.

477
00:40:10,500 --> 00:40:13,700
Over 80 cameras are strategically placed throughout the structure.

478
00:40:13,701 --> 00:40:20,040
Some of them are along the walls, some of them might be on light poles, or others might be right in the towers.

479
00:40:20,820 --> 00:40:26,340
It helps us expedite the proper personnel to any type of incidents, which makes everything more efficient.

480
00:40:27,500 --> 00:40:31,360
All the sensor and camera information is relayed to the bridge's control center

481
00:40:31,361 --> 00:40:38,700
and allows bridge personnel to manage the traffic flow via lighted signs and radio broadcasts.

482
00:40:38,701 --> 00:40:45,700
Even toll collection on the bridge has undergone an electronic makeover.

483
00:40:45,701 --> 00:40:50,700
For years, drivers lined up at the toll booths to pay their $6 crossing fee.

484
00:40:50,701 --> 00:40:57,700
Today, thanks to an electronic tag known as an E-ZPass, the cars are scanned and charged automatically.

485
00:40:57,701 --> 00:41:02,700
But the E-ZPass tags do more than speed motorists through the toll booths.

486
00:41:02,701 --> 00:41:08,700
Since traffic moves in both directions on each level of the bridge, motorists have to make a choice.

487
00:41:08,701 --> 00:41:14,700
The real-time information collected by the EZPass helps drivers pick the level that is moving the quickest.

488
00:41:15,540 --> 00:41:19,080
The EZPass tags are part of our intelligent transportation system.

489
00:41:19,480 --> 00:41:22,660
By using them as probes in the system with a computer algorithm,

490
00:41:23,060 --> 00:41:28,080
we can know how long it takes to travel between two different points by the number of tags that traverse that point.

491
00:41:28,500 --> 00:41:31,720
So we can tell our customers if you take the route X or you take route Y,

492
00:41:31,800 --> 00:41:34,900
it'll be a certain amount of minutes and might be faster or slower.

493
00:41:34,901 --> 00:41:39,660
And in turn, when the customer sees that come up on variable message signs as they approach our roadways,

494
00:41:39,661 --> 00:41:42,180
they believe us because they know it's done in real time.

495
00:41:42,320 --> 00:41:45,020
And it's like we're reaching into the car and grabbing the steering wheel.

496
00:41:45,340 --> 00:41:46,920
The customers will go the shortest route.

497
00:41:48,060 --> 00:41:52,040
But for the Port Authority, there are larger concerns than just keeping the traffic moving.

498
00:41:53,240 --> 00:41:56,700
As with all major civil engineering facilities since 9-11,

499
00:41:57,600 --> 00:41:59,960
security has become a top priority on the GW.

500
00:41:59,961 --> 00:42:06,960
additional personnel have been hired to stand guard 24-7 at sensitive locations

501
00:42:06,961 --> 00:42:10,960
like the tower foundations and anchorages

502
00:42:10,961 --> 00:42:15,520
cameras once used only to monitor traffic flow also keep an electronic eye

503
00:42:15,521 --> 00:42:19,020
out for any suspicious activity

504
00:42:19,021 --> 00:42:23,360
we've also gotten a security system which is part of the technology advances

505
00:42:23,361 --> 00:42:28,780
that we've made there's 900 points on the facility that are monitored 24 hours

506
00:42:28,781 --> 00:42:34,240
hours a day, seven days a week, to ensure that doors are not opened when they're not

507
00:42:34,241 --> 00:42:38,260
supposed to, that people aren't accessing areas that are critical to the operation of

508
00:42:38,261 --> 00:42:43,000
the facility.

509
00:42:43,001 --> 00:42:45,758
But the bridge's enemies aren't necessarily human.

510
00:42:45,759 --> 00:42:48,240
Regular maintenance and long-term renovations

511
00:42:48,241 --> 00:42:50,980
are part of the plan to combat the forces of nature.

512
00:42:50,981 --> 00:42:57,340
To give you an idea of what we do in terms of maintenance, we have ongoing right now

513
00:42:57,341 --> 00:43:00,100
probably 20 projects in construction.

514
00:43:00,860 --> 00:43:05,320
We're spending anywhere from 30 to 50 million dollars

515
00:43:05,321 --> 00:43:07,960
over the last three to four years

516
00:43:07,961 --> 00:43:10,700
in terms of construction out at the bridge,

517
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:14,180
in terms of what we're designing and in terms of planning.

518
00:43:14,620 --> 00:43:18,160
We have close to 30 projects that we have under design right now.

519
00:43:18,760 --> 00:43:20,820
One of the biggest projects on the docket?

520
00:43:21,520 --> 00:43:25,900
In 2006, the Port Authority will begin to build ramps on the New Jersey side,

521
00:43:25,901 --> 00:43:30,380
connecting the Palisades Interstate Parkway with the lower level of the bridge.

522
00:43:30,980 --> 00:43:34,820
The construction costs for the massive undertaking are estimated at $60 million.

523
00:43:35,900 --> 00:43:38,440
The ramp should be open to traffic by 2010.

524
00:43:39,920 --> 00:43:43,620
And any engineer that works on this, you have a sense of pride.

525
00:43:44,060 --> 00:43:46,020
We have a sense of ownership of all of our facilities,

526
00:43:46,180 --> 00:43:47,820
but particularly the George Washington Bridge,

527
00:43:47,880 --> 00:43:50,380
because you go anywhere around the country, anywhere around the world,

528
00:43:50,840 --> 00:43:53,900
it's a recognized landmark, and it really gives you a sense of pride

529
00:43:53,901 --> 00:43:56,080
to be able to work on a structure like that.

530
00:43:56,960 --> 00:43:59,740
For those entering New York City for the first time,

531
00:44:00,600 --> 00:44:03,480
there are other monuments that may be more identifiable.

532
00:44:04,160 --> 00:44:05,900
But in a city of great bridges,

533
00:44:06,360 --> 00:44:08,460
the George Washington has distinguished itself

534
00:44:08,461 --> 00:44:11,900
with those who depend on it most, New Yorkers.

535
00:44:13,640 --> 00:44:16,040
For New Yorkers, the George Washington Bridge

536
00:44:16,041 --> 00:44:18,000
is a foremost icon

537
00:44:18,001 --> 00:44:21,300
because when they see those soaring steel towers,

538
00:44:21,301 --> 00:44:23,160
they know that they're almost home.

